WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:04.675
Two rooms share a wall

2
00:00:04.675 --> 00:00:07.375
and each has walls to the outside.

3
00:00:07.453 --> 00:00:09.845
Measure both temperatures above

4
00:00:09.922 --> 00:00:11.774
the outside temperature

5
00:00:11.774 --> 00:00:14.860
and make the pair a point in the plane.

6
00:00:15.210 --> 00:00:18.888
Each room loses heat outside at rate two

7
00:00:18.978 --> 00:00:21.310
and heat flows through the

8
00:00:21.400 --> 00:00:25.257
shared wall at rate one, from warm to cool.

9
00:00:25.257 --> 00:00:29.204
That gives a matrix: minus three, one, one

10
00:00:29.204 --> 00:00:30.370
minus three.

11
00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:32.654
Room one is heated to ten

12
00:00:32.731 --> 00:00:34.433
degrees above outside

13
00:00:34.433 --> 00:00:37.527
room two is at the outside temperature

14
00:00:37.527 --> 00:00:40.080
and the heating is switched off.

15
00:00:40.430 --> 00:00:43.855
What does room two do first: warm up

16
00:00:43.855 --> 00:00:45.151
or cool down?

17
00:00:45.151 --> 00:00:46.910
Pause and predict.

18
00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:54.758
The equation says how fast the point moves.

19
00:00:54.758 --> 00:00:57.437
Its velocity is A times T.

20
00:00:57.437 --> 00:01:01.407
At ten, zero that is minus thirty, ten

21
00:01:01.407 --> 00:01:03.690
left, and a little up.

22
00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.176
Up means room two warms.

23
00:01:06.265 --> 00:01:09.557
Heat flows in through the wall before

24
00:01:09.646 --> 00:01:11.960
the outside takes it away.

25
00:01:12.310 --> 00:01:14.674
Now try the point four, four

26
00:01:14.755 --> 00:01:17.038
its velocity points straight

27
00:01:17.119 --> 00:01:18.668
back at the origin.

28
00:01:18.668 --> 00:01:22.092
And three, minus three: also straight in

29
00:01:22.092 --> 00:01:23.070
and faster.

30
00:01:23.650 --> 00:01:26.230
Those are the eigenvectors.

31
00:01:26.230 --> 00:01:29.574
One, one has eigenvalue minus two

32
00:01:29.669 --> 00:01:32.345
equal rooms exchange nothing

33
00:01:32.440 --> 00:01:35.020
and only lose heat outside.

34
00:01:35.020 --> 00:01:37.982
One, minus one has minus four

35
00:01:37.982 --> 00:01:41.995
a difference also leaks through the wall

36
00:01:41.995 --> 00:01:43.810
so it dies faster.

37
00:01:44.160 --> 00:01:46.895
On an eigenvector the solution

38
00:01:46.986 --> 00:01:49.721
is e to the lambda t, times x.

39
00:01:49.721 --> 00:01:53.276
Its derivative is lambda times itself

40
00:01:53.276 --> 00:01:55.920
which is exactly A times it.

41
00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:58.112
Let a quarter hour pass.

42
00:01:58.112 --> 00:02:00.492
Both arrows slide straight in

43
00:02:00.492 --> 00:02:02.334
the second much faster.

44
00:02:02.334 --> 00:02:04.023
Another quarter hour

45
00:02:04.023 --> 00:02:06.710
and the difference is almost gone.

46
00:02:07.290 --> 00:02:09.201
Back to ten, zero.

47
00:02:09.201 --> 00:02:12.704
Split it along the eigenvectors

48
00:02:12.704 --> 00:02:17.162
five times one, one, plus five times one

49
00:02:17.162 --> 00:02:18.330
minus one.

50
00:02:18.680 --> 00:02:21.277
Each part shrinks at its own rate

51
00:02:21.277 --> 00:02:24.562
five e to the minus two t along the first

52
00:02:24.562 --> 00:02:28.000
five e to the minus four t along the second.

53
00:02:28.350 --> 00:02:29.686
Run the clock.

54
00:02:29.686 --> 00:02:32.168
After a tenth of an hour.

55
00:02:32.168 --> 00:02:34.268
After a quarter hour.

56
00:02:34.268 --> 00:02:36.177
After half an hour.

57
00:02:36.177 --> 00:02:37.990
After a full hour.

58
00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:40.850
The difference part dies first

59
00:02:40.850 --> 00:02:43.845
so the path bends onto the diagonal

60
00:02:43.845 --> 00:02:46.274
and both rooms cool together

61
00:02:46.274 --> 00:02:48.460
like e to the minus two t.

62
00:02:49.040 --> 00:02:51.178
When is room two warmest?

63
00:02:51.263 --> 00:02:54.085
Its rate of change is zero when e

64
00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:56.821
to the minus two t is one half

65
00:02:56.821 --> 00:03:00.497
at a half of the natural log of two hours

66
00:03:00.497 --> 00:03:02.720
about twenty-one minutes.

67
00:03:03.070 --> 00:03:06.558
Start the clock again and stop it there.

68
00:03:06.645 --> 00:03:09.087
Room one has fallen to three

69
00:03:09.174 --> 00:03:10.657
point seven five

70
00:03:10.657 --> 00:03:14.319
room two has risen to one point two five

71
00:03:14.319 --> 00:03:17.110
and from now on both only cool.

72
00:03:17.690 --> 00:03:21.412
A tempting shortcut: we have two solutions

73
00:03:21.412 --> 00:03:22.970
so just add them?

74
00:03:23.550 --> 00:03:28.207
At time zero that sum is one, one plus one

75
00:03:28.207 --> 00:03:30.590
minus one: two, zero.

76
00:03:30.590 --> 00:03:34.488
But the rooms started at ten, zero.

77
00:03:34.488 --> 00:03:37.087
It solves the equation

78
00:03:37.087 --> 00:03:39.470
just not our problem.

79
00:03:39.820 --> 00:03:42.999
The coefficients come from the start

80
00:03:42.999 --> 00:03:46.521
X inverse times T of zero is five, five.

81
00:03:46.521 --> 00:03:48.755
Scale both parts by five

82
00:03:48.755 --> 00:03:51.504
and the sum lands on ten, zero.

83
00:03:51.504 --> 00:03:54.940
Always check the solution at time zero.

84
00:03:55.520 --> 00:03:57.443
Without any formula

85
00:03:57.539 --> 00:03:59.942
a computer can follow the

86
00:04:00.038 --> 00:04:02.345
equation in small steps

87
00:04:02.345 --> 00:04:04.364
over a short time h

88
00:04:04.364 --> 00:04:07.440
T changes by about h times A T.

89
00:04:07.790 --> 00:04:09.647
With h equal to a tenth

90
00:04:09.647 --> 00:04:11.659
the steps are too coarse.

91
00:04:11.659 --> 00:04:14.676
One step, a second step, a third step

92
00:04:14.753 --> 00:04:17.152
the polygon cuts below the true

93
00:04:17.229 --> 00:04:19.550
curve and drifts away from it.

94
00:04:19.900 --> 00:04:22.680
With h equal to one ten-thousandth

95
00:04:22.759 --> 00:04:25.380
ten thousand steps agree with the

96
00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:27.525
formula to three decimals.

97
00:04:27.525 --> 00:04:30.940
The eigenvectors simply skip the stepping.

98
00:04:31.520 --> 00:04:35.133
So, to sum up: on an eigenvector the state

99
00:04:35.219 --> 00:04:38.746
slides straight in, as e to the lambda t.

100
00:04:38.746 --> 00:04:42.187
Split the start along the eigenvectors

101
00:04:42.187 --> 00:04:45.800
and let each part shrink at its own rate.

102
00:04:46.150 --> 00:04:48.474
The fast part dies first

103
00:04:48.567 --> 00:04:50.705
and the path bends onto

104
00:04:50.798 --> 00:04:52.750
the slow eigenvector.

105
00:04:53.330 --> 00:04:56.717
In part two: when solutions grow instead

106
00:04:56.800 --> 00:04:58.865
and what happens when the

107
00:04:58.948 --> 00:05:00.930
eigenvalues are complex.

108
00:05:00.930 --> 00:05:03.739
Then the point does not slide in

109
00:05:03.739 --> 00:05:04.730
it spirals.
